d — that aligns.\n\nSo options:\n- Check (0): lets opponent continue with control; may face another bet.\n- Raise to 500 total (i.e., add 400): shows strength, protects against draws, but risks building a pot with a vulnerable hand.\n- All-in (-2): you have 19,800 chips, so going all-in would be a massive bluff or value overplay — very risky given your hand strength.\n- Fold (-1): folding two pair is generally too tight unless you're certain you're beaten.\n\nGiven your chip stack (19,800), you can afford some caution. Since you're in the lead cumulatively and near the end of the 20-hand game, preserving equity matters.\n\nBut right now, **two pair (9s and 4s)** is likely ahead of many hands that don’t contain a 9 or a higher pair. However, the opponent’s raise suggests they may have a 9 or a strong pocket pair.\n\nBest line: **Check (0)**.  \nWhy? Because you’ve already faced a raise on the flop. If you check now, it looks like weakness, which might induce a bluff from the opponent on the turn if a scare card comes. Alternatively, if you raise, you risk getting re-raised and being forced to fold anyway — and only worse hands (like sets or better two pair) will call, so your hand has little upside.\n\nChecking keeps the pot manageable and allows you to see the turn card and react.\n\nReason: Checking maintains pot control with a medium-strength hand. Raising risks bloating the pot when you may already be behind or drawing dead. Folding two pair is too weak given the board texture and history.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
